<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odenwald</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Odenwald is a wooded upland region in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> plain, the Main and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period it was divided among the Electoral Palatinate, the Counts of Erbach, the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and other lords, and it is known for its castles and the Roman frontier line that crossed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odenwald.jpg" alt="Odenwald" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thoroe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odenwald_-_Deutsche_Mittelgebirge,_Serie_A-de.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
